WEBVTT

00:00:00.536 --> 00:00:23.960
<v Casey>Hey, welcome back. On today's episode: museum staff at the V&amp;A walking out over "Amazon-style" conditions, a protest hitting the British Museum over who bankrolls the Bayeux Tapestry exhibit, and a provincial campaign with a strange local blind spot. Plus our Deep Dive question — is a tiny volunteer-run gallery actually safer than a giant institution, or just one bad year from folding? That and more, coming right up.

00:00:37.435 --> 00:00:57.699
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Casey. It's Monday, September 28th, 2026. Speaking to you from the traditional territories of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations here in the Cariboo region. Today's theme is Arts, Culture &amp; Digital Storytelling.

00:00:58.435 --> 00:01:13.595
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. We're looking at who gets to tell the story of a place and what it costs to keep telling it — festivals, galleries, museums, community radio, all of it, and what happens when the people running it are three volunteers and a grant application.

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:29.494
<v Casey>Quick weather check before we get into it. Out at Horsefly Lake this morning it's partly cloudy and around 7 degrees, with a high of 14 and a low of 5. There's drizzle moving through the region today — 74 percent chance of precipitation, about 2 millimetres.

00:01:30.096 --> 00:01:45.448
<v Riley>Around the rest of the Cariboo, 100 Mile House and Williams Lake are both sitting near 5 degrees with partly cloudy skies. Quesnel's a touch warmer at 7 but overcast, and Redstone is around 5, also overcast. Bring a jacket if you're heading out.

00:01:45.814 --> 00:01:47.214
<v Casey>Let's get into what's happening.

00:01:48.613 --> 00:01:58.685
<v Casey>A fair amount of today's news is institutions under pressure — museums, a provincial campaign, workers pushing back on the conditions they're asked to work under. Close to home first.

00:02:00.036 --> 00:02:32.340
<v Riley>The Mounties are out with a reminder that's more practical than it sounds. RCMP detachments across the Cariboo are asking hunters heading into the bush to be mindful of firearm storage and transport, particularly during quick stops or overnight stays in town — locking firearms out of sight, storing ammunition separately, the basics. The line from the release was blunt: they want to, in their words, "prevent the loss of a buck," meaning the rifle, not the animal. A stolen firearm out of a parked truck is a policing headache that follows for years.

00:02:33.024 --> 00:02:35.560
<v Casey>Bad pun, much worse consequence if it happens.

00:02:37.201 --> 00:03:12.585
<v Casey>From there to the provincial campaign, which landed at our own doorstep in an odd way this weekend. B.C. Conservative leader Lorne Doerkson brought a "no new taxes" fiscal message to a campaign stop in Surrey, standing alongside candidates and supporters there. That's notable for us because Doerkson is our own sitting MLA for Cariboo-Chilcotin. He was first elected in 2020 under the B.C. Liberal banner, winning just over 51 percent of the vote, and was re-elected in 2024 as a B.C. Conservative with roughly 69 percent against the NDP candidate. And now he's not just the local MLA — he's the party leader.

00:03:13.793 --> 00:03:53.553
<v Riley>He was named interim leader of the B.C. Conservatives following Kerry-Lynne Findlay's resignation, and the snap election was called less than 48 hours after that appointment — CTV reported that timeline directly. The 100 Mile Free Press ran a piece calling him a "former rodeo rider" seeking to "buck off" Premier Eby in this election, which is a good headline whatever you think of the politics. So he's out fighting for the province's confidence in Surrey, while his own riding hasn't publicly produced a single filed candidate that we've been able to source — not him, not anyone else. Nominations across the province stay open until October 3rd.

00:03:54.663 --> 00:04:03.319
<v Casey>A strange gap to sit with — the riding's own representative is suddenly the face of the entire campaign, and locally we don't know who's contesting the seat.

00:04:04.634 --> 00:04:25.666
<v Riley>The granular tracking is happening in outlets like My Cariboo Now and the 100 Mile Free Press — they carried the vote-margin breakdowns from 2024 and they're following the local mechanics the bigger Vancouver papers skip past on their way to the leaders' tour. Hyperlocal digital coverage is doing work nobody else is bothering to do.

00:04:27.072 --> 00:04:53.800
<v Casey>Same story plays out next door in Prince George-North Cariboo, which includes Quesnel. Sheldon Clare holds that seat, and his own path to the leadership stumbled on something almost embarrassingly mundane. He ran for the top job earlier this year, and the party's Leadership Election Organizing Committee found that a critical number of his roughly 250 submitted nomination signatures were invalid — people who weren't party members, or whose memberships had lapsed.

00:04:54.329 --> 00:04:56.537
<v Riley>An unglamorous way to lose a leadership bid.

00:04:57.811 --> 00:06:00.171
<v Casey>Clare said in his own statement that he took, quote, "full responsibility for that deficiency in my application," while adding he remained proud of the ideas he'd advanced. He withdrew from the race. Fellow MLA Steve Kooner also withdrew that same week, saying it was to help unite the party. Nine candidates remained after that, including sitting MLAs Peter Milobar and Bruce Banman, plus Harman Bhangu, Iain Black, Caroline Elliott, Kerry-Lynne Findlay, Yuri Fulmer, Warren Hamm, and Darrell Jones. Clare himself said publicly that the new contest rules were meant to stop what he called "any funny business," after the prior year's leadership review was, in his words, marred by controversy — that's his characterization, not an independent finding. Findlay won that race with over 61 percent support in the first round in the Quesnel and Prince George-North Cariboo area specifically, beating Caroline Elliott, who finished second at just over 38 percent. A few months later, Findlay resigned and Doerkson stepped into the interim leadership. No candidates confirmed for Clare's own riding either, as of today.

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:23.344
<v Riley>The other half of that same Surrey weekend belonged to the NDP. David Eby held a press conference at Peace Arch Park focused on recruiting American health workers south of the border to fill staffing gaps in B.C.'s system, flanked by other NDP candidates and supporters. It was framed around border politics and health staffing rather than anything riding-specific to us.

00:06:24.673 --> 00:06:29.481
<v Casey>Same city, same weekend, two party leaders, and neither one talking directly about the Cariboo.

00:06:30.843 --> 00:06:52.667
<v Riley>From the campaign trail to museum picket lines. Staff across the Victoria and Albert Museum's London locations walked out for two days earlier this month, shuttering three sites entirely. Unionized workers cited what they called "Amazon-style" working conditions — the kind of operational pressure you'd associate with a warehouse, not a national museum.

00:06:54.045 --> 00:07:00.085
<v Casey>A museum with royal patronage and centuries of collections, and the floor staff say they're managed like a distribution centre.

00:07:01.595 --> 00:07:28.403
<v Casey>There was a related protest around the same time at the British Museum, where demonstrators targeted a Bayeux Tapestry exhibition sponsor over financial ties to Israeli settlements — specifically, billionaire Igor Tulchinsky's financing of a community centre in an illegal Israeli settlement in the Occupied West Bank. Different grievance from the V&amp;A strike, same underlying pattern of institutions answering, or failing to answer, for the money that keeps their doors open.

00:07:30.019 --> 00:07:51.627
<v Casey>A smaller and stranger story about who actually controls a shared space online. Middle schoolers, likely blocked from the apps and platforms everyone else uses, apparently discovered they could turn the comment section under an NPR podcast episode — Wild Card, hosted on Spotify — into an impromptu group chat. Ira Glass dug into how they landed there on a new episode of This American Life.

00:07:52.829 --> 00:07:59.021
<v Riley>Kids will always find the unlocked door, even if it's a podcast comment thread nobody expected them to use that way.

00:08:00.613 --> 00:08:28.757
<v Riley>From there, a different kind of access question — heritage you can visit without ever leaving home. ArchDaily covered a virtual reality reconstruction of the Ajanta caves in Maharashtra, letting visitors move through a digitally rebuilt version of a site most people will never travel to see in person. The piece also names the limit: a replica isn't the site. It can't reproduce the weathering, the temperature, the scale you only get standing inside the rock.

00:08:30.129 --> 00:08:34.593
<v Casey>Digital access is real access — it's just a different thing than the object itself.

00:08:36.601 --> 00:08:52.089
<v Riley>On the maker side, Hackaday wrote up someone who modified a center-pivot irrigation rig — the giant circular arms you see from a plane flying over farmland — into a functioning lawnmower, repurposing agricultural hardware most people drive past without a second thought.

00:08:53.518 --> 00:08:58.382
<v Casey>The kind of fix a Cariboo rancher rigged up in a shed years ago and never bothered blogging about.

00:08:59.595 --> 00:09:22.099
<v Riley>A randomized trial called SINGLE-AF found direct oral anticoagulants cut serious clinical events by 69 percent in atrial fibrillation patients at intermediate stroke risk, driven mainly by fewer ischemic strokes, with no increase in major bleeding. Not our usual beat, but a meaningful number for anyone managing that condition, or with a family member who is.

00:09:24.095 --> 00:09:41.615
<v Casey>On the smaller and more personal end, the blogger Julia Evans wrote about replacing a dead battery in a set of rechargeable bike lights she'd bought a decade ago and hadn't used in years. The fix took her longer than buying a new pair would have, but she got the old ones running again instead of leaving them in a drawer for good.

00:09:42.849 --> 00:09:48.481
<v Riley>A small, stubborn win for repair over replacement, which is a philosophy this show has some sympathy for.

00:09:50.442 --> 00:10:09.202
<v Casey>On the software side, XDA Developers found that changing a single Windows 11 setting — turning down some of the built-in visual effects — noticeably speeds up performance on older, lower-end hardware. Useful if you're running a machine at the library, the office, or home that's a few years past its prime and you're not ready to replace it.

00:10:10.639 --> 00:10:30.143
<v Riley>Cineplex may be headed toward a sale, and CBC Arts reports something that cuts against the obvious narrative — audiences appear to be rediscovering the actual theatre experience, and the company's own financial reporting backs that up, driven in part by younger moviegoers showing up in numbers nobody quite predicted.

00:10:31.637 --> 00:10:36.301
<v Casey>After years of "streaming killed the cinema" takes, that's a surprising contradiction to land on.

00:10:37.774 --> 00:10:58.350
<v Riley>Two health stories before we wrap. Researchers used trimodal ultrasound imaging to watch a glioblastoma tumor physically reshape a mouse's brain as it grew, tracking the tumor, the blood vessels, and functional brain activity all at once — described as a major step forward in studying how these cancers develop, not just where they end up sitting.

00:10:59.592 --> 00:11:15.680
<v Casey>And NPR followed Bryant Lin, a Stanford medical professor who spent his career advocating for better lung cancer screening — then became a Stage IV patient of that very disease two years ago. The piece follows him navigating treatment from inside the field he'd studied from the outside.

00:11:16.880 --> 00:11:38.008
<v Riley>Last story before the correction. Britain's next-generation Challenger 3 main battle tank is reportedly being built with what Business Insider describes as a "load of data" pulled directly from the war in Ukraine — real combat lessons from an active conflict shaping the design specifications of hardware that won't roll into service for years yet.

00:11:39.455 --> 00:11:46.687
<v Casey>Its own strange form of storytelling — a battlefield writing the specs for a machine before the war that produced the data has finished.

00:11:48.339 --> 00:12:14.243
<v Casey>One correction. In our episode on September 26th, we said municipal election nominations for Williams Lake and the Cariboo Regional District closed October 1st. That date belongs to a separate, unrelated deadline — nominations for the 29th annual community award mentioned in the same story. The municipal election nomination period for the October 17th vote closed back on September 12th. We caught that one ourselves, in production.

00:12:15.417 --> 00:12:26.409
<v Riley>Two dates in one sentence, easy to cross wires on, and we'll be slower with it next time. Speaking of people keeping the local record straight — that's where the spotlight goes today.

00:12:27.809 --> 00:12:58.161
<v Riley>Culture Days is running across the province, and the Central Cariboo Arts &amp; Culture Society is right in the middle of it. They manage the Arts Centre, run the local grants program, and put on the Performances in the Park summer concert series with paid artists, paid sound techs, and paid staff behind it. The Culture Days stretch means open studios, hands-on workshops, and free events around the Central Cariboo — a working ceramics studio or a printmaking table, up close, with nobody expecting you to buy anything.

00:12:58.828 --> 00:13:05.604
<v Casey>Free access to a working studio is rare enough that I'd take them up on it — you rarely get to see the mess behind the finished thing.

00:13:06.158 --> 00:13:20.110
<v Riley>Which sets up the thing worth digging into properly — small arts organizations running on a handful of volunteers, versus enormous institutions with unions and centuries of collections behind them, and which one is actually more fragile.

00:13:21.510 --> 00:13:43.806
<v Casey>Start with the question underneath all of it. Staff at the V&amp;A are striking over conditions, and museums across Europe are being robbed by people who are in and out fast. So is a tiny volunteer-run gallery up here the safer bet, because it's small and personal and nobody's running it like a warehouse? Or is "small and volunteer-run" just the polite phrase for underfunded and one bad year from folding?

00:13:44.110 --> 00:14:04.518
<v Riley>The scale argument cuts both ways, and that's the part nobody wants to sit in. The V&amp;A has centuries of institutional weight, a payroll department, a whole management layer — and its own staff still walked out calling the conditions "Amazon-style." Being big doesn't buy you good management. It buys you more people around to be disappointed by it.

00:14:05.109 --> 00:14:21.413
<v Casey>Big also buys you a security budget. The Hyperallergic piece on the European theft wave makes the point without much subtlety: these institutions are struggling to protect irreplaceable collections after years of underfunding, and the thefts land hardest on the places that aren't flagships.

00:14:21.697 --> 00:14:25.753
<v Riley>The Louvre got robbed too, and that's not an under-resourced institution.

00:14:26.387 --> 00:14:41.395
<v Casey>Once, and it made global news for a week. That piece is explicitly about the thefts that are bigger than the Louvre story — the ones at smaller and mid-tier museums that never crack international headlines, because nobody's photographing the aftermath for the wire services.

00:14:42.070 --> 00:14:59.638
<v Riley>Small can move faster on the exact things that prevent theft, though — better locks, a tighter rotation schedule, knowing every volunteer by name and face. The V&amp;A can't retrain its entire security staff overnight. A three-person board can change the locks by Thursday if they decide it matters.

00:14:59.954 --> 00:15:01.234
<v Casey>Can they afford the locks?

00:15:01.508 --> 00:15:18.740
<v Riley>Yes, and that's the underrated part. Decent hardware-store locks and a couple of cameras are a hardware-store expense, not a security-consultancy retainer. The financial barrier at the small end is much lower than people assume — the barrier is usually the decision, not the invoice.

00:15:19.351 --> 00:15:31.479
<v Casey>The barrier is that a volunteer board running things off the side of their own desks won't conduct a security audit unless something's already gone wrong. Nobody schedules the fire drill for the fire that hasn't happened.

00:15:32.039 --> 00:15:53.183
<v Riley>Reactive versus proactive — and small organizations skew reactive because no one's job description says "think about this before it's a problem." Which is why the fix I'd push isn't a consultant. A regional museum running on volunteers needs a complete photographic inventory of its own collection, held somewhere other than the building itself.

00:15:53.471 --> 00:15:55.351
<v Casey>Why does that beat the lock on the door?

00:15:55.689 --> 00:16:15.305
<v Riley>Because recovery odds after a theft depend almost entirely on documentation good enough to match against something like Interpol's stolen art database, or against an insurance claim. Plenty of small museums don't have that in usable form — not because it's expensive, but because nobody's ever been assigned to sit down and do it.

00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:40.070
<v Casey>Assigning a task to a volunteer board doesn't make the hours materialize. And the Bayeux Tapestry protest is a different vulnerability entirely — that wasn't a broken lock, it was a governance failure over who gets to attach their name and money to a cultural object. Tulchinsky's financing tied to a settlement in the Occupied West Bank wasn't a security question. It was whether anyone checks before the sponsorship deal is signed.

00:16:40.666 --> 00:16:58.930
<v Riley>Same question underneath both, though — who has the authority to decide what's acceptable, and does anyone check before the money's spent and the exhibit's hung. And a small organization is where that authority is clearest. There's no committee to hide behind. Three people know exactly who said yes.

00:16:59.495 --> 00:17:17.527
<v Casey>Clarity isn't leverage. The V&amp;A can lose a sponsor over bad press without blinking. A gallery running on three volunteers can't, so "we know who said yes" mostly tells you who had no choice. Small is more accountable only if the small organization can afford to refuse money it needs. Otherwise it isn't principled, it's cornered.

00:17:18.165 --> 00:17:40.525
<v Riley>Which is where Surrey is instructive. The province and the city just settled that Surrey will host the long-awaited B.C. South Asian heritage museum, and the two of them now work together to find a site. That's what political will plus a real budget line produces — staffing and security and provenance planning built in at the start instead of bolted on after a scare.

00:17:41.107 --> 00:18:00.491
<v Casey>They have a winning bid and no building. "Now work together to find a site" is the polite phase of a project, the one before land costs and consultation and a change of government. Big projects fail differently than small ones — they fail slowly, in procurement, with everyone still employed. I wouldn't hold that up as the finished counterexample yet.

00:18:01.166 --> 00:18:20.238
<v Riley>Fine, it's unbuilt. It's also a museum that exists on a provincial ledger, which is more than most community collections up here can say. And "whoever showed up and stayed" describes a huge share of the infrastructure in this region, arts or otherwise, and most of it has worked for decades without a budget line.

00:18:20.775 --> 00:18:35.871
<v Casey>Until the one person who understood the alarm system retires and nobody was ever taught it. That's how small institutions actually fail — not with a fire or a break-in on the news, just quietly, one missing person at a time, until the gap shows up after something's already gone.

00:18:36.456 --> 00:18:45.912
<v Riley>So the dividing line isn't size. It's whether the institutional knowledge lives in one person's head or is written down somewhere two other people could find it.

00:18:46.524 --> 00:19:04.436
<v Casey>That's closer to the real distinction than big versus small. A small operation can be nimble and well-run. It can also be one retirement away from losing the only person who knew where the provenance file lives — and I promise you it's in a binder, and I promise you the binder is in the same building as the collection.

00:19:04.799 --> 00:19:07.647
<v Riley>Next to the fire extinguisher it's meant to survive.

00:19:07.967 --> 00:19:10.311
<v Casey>Exactly. Fireproof plan, flammable location.

00:19:10.826 --> 00:19:28.194
<v Riley>And it's testable, which is the part I like. Does a small gallery or archive up here have a photographic inventory of its collection stored off-site, separate from the building? That's not a grant application question. It's a yes-or-no operational fact you could establish by Friday.

00:19:28.752 --> 00:19:37.480
<v Casey>And if the answer's no, in most cases that isn't a funding gap. That's an afternoon with a decent camera and a hard drive that lives at somebody's house across town.

00:19:38.081 --> 00:20:02.409
<v Riley>Which is what a Culture Days weekend is good for, past the studio tours — using the foot traffic to ask the people running the space whether that inventory exists, and if not, whether a volunteer with a phone could knock it out this month. Digital storytelling isn't only the polished VR reconstruction of a cave in Maharashtra. Sometimes it's four hundred flash photos of a collection, backed up twice.

00:20:03.071 --> 00:20:14.367
<v Casey>The unglamorous version is usually the one that survives. And I'd take that question over another appeal for money, because the answer tells you something true about how the place is run rather than about its bank balance.

00:20:14.906 --> 00:20:49.778
<v Riley>So the test for whether small is a virtue or an excuse up here comes down to whether the knowledge inside an organization is recorded anywhere besides one person's memory — and the good news is that's cheap, fast, and entirely within reach of a three-person board. If you run or volunteer with a small gallery, museum, or archive anywhere in the Cariboo and want to talk through what basic documentation could look like at your size, or if you've already got that off-site copy sorted, write in — feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a. We'd like to be proven wrong on this one.

00:20:50.403 --> 00:21:05.395
<v Casey>I'd like to be wrong too, though my honest bet is a lot of "we've been meaning to." What shifted for me is the cost argument — I came in assuming this was a money problem, and it mostly isn't. What hasn't shifted is who does the work, because nobody's paid to notice.

00:21:05.717 --> 00:21:09.269
<v Riley>So go check on a hard drive somewhere, and have a good Monday.

00:21:09.449 --> 00:21:10.169
<v Casey>See you tomorrow.